{
  "schema": "qauth-vectors/1-rc.0",
  "id": "dpop/proof",
  "description": "DPoP proof checks a server runs on the DPoP header of a request (RFC 9449 §4.3). Each case presents input.requests in order to one verifier that starts with an empty replay store, with input.now (Unix seconds) as the current time. A request has dpop, the list of its DPoP header values (an empty list is no header); method and url, the method of the request and the absolute URL it was sent to; and, at a protected resource, access_token, the access token presented with it. The verifier accepts the algorithms ES256 and EdDSA only, and an iat at most 60 seconds from input.now in either direction, edges included. When input.nonce is present the server requires a server-provided nonce and accepts exactly the values in input.nonce.accepted; when it is absent the server provides none and does not check a nonce claim. A proof passes when there is exactly one DPoP header and it is a compact JWS; typ is exactly \"dpop+jwt\"; alg is on the allowlist; the jwk header is a public key of the type alg needs, with no private or symmetric key members, and the signature verifies under it; jti is a non-empty string; htm equals method exactly; htu equals url after RFC 3986 syntax- and scheme-based normalization, ignoring the query and fragment of both; iat is a number within the window; when access_token is present, ath equals the base64url SHA-256 of its ASCII bytes; when a nonce is required, the nonce claim is one of the accepted values; and no earlier request of the case was accepted with a proof of the same key and jti. expected.results has one entry per request: {\"valid\": true, \"jkt\": ...}, where jkt is the RFC 7638 SHA-256 thumbprint of the jwk, base64url; {\"valid\": false, \"error\": \"use_dpop_nonce\"} when the proof passes every other check and its nonce is missing or not accepted; otherwise {\"valid\": false, \"error\": \"invalid_dpop_proof\"}. The rfc9449- cases use the example proofs of RFC 9449 Figures 2, 7 and 13, signed with the RFC's own public example key, which has no kid. Every other proof is signed with a throwaway Ed25519 key generated from a fixed seed for this file, whose jwk says so in its kid; jwk-private-key carries that throwaway key's private member d in its header on purpose, and alg-hs256-symmetric-key a throwaway symmetric key.",
  "source": "RFC 9449 §4.2, §4.3, §7, §8 and §11.1, Figures 2, 7, 9, 13, 20, 21 and 23; generated by test-vectors/generators/dpop-proof.mjs with Node.js node:crypto",
